    "# Quiz: Build a Web App to use a ML Model\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-3-1-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What does ONNX stand for?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Over Neural Network Exchange</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. ONNX stands for Open Neural Network Exchange.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Open Neural Network Exchange</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. ONNX stands for Open Neural Network Exchange, a format for using models in JavaScript web apps.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Output Neural Network Exchange</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The O in ONNX stands for Open, not Output.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-3-1-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>How is Flask defined by its creators?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>mini-framework</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Flask's creators call it a 'micro-framework', not a mini-framework.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>large-framework</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Flask's creators call it a 'micro-framework'. It provides the basic features of web frameworks.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>micro-framework</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Flask's creators call it a 'micro-framework'. It gives the basic features of web frameworks using Python.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-3-1-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What does the Pickle module of Python do</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Serializes a Python Object</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Pickle does serialize objects, but it also de-serializes them.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>De-serializes a Python Object</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Pickle does de-serialize objects, but it also serializes them.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Serializes and De-serializes a Python Object</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Pickle both serializes and de-serializes Python objects, such as a trained model.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-3-1-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What are the tools we can use to host a pre-trained model on the web using Python?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Flask</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Flask runs on Python, and it is used to build the web app that calls the pickled model.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>TensorFlow.js</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. TensorFlow.js is used in a JavaScript context. The Python tool for the web app is Flask.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>onnx.js</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The ONNX format is for JavaScript web apps. The Python tool for the web app is Flask.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
